00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:07.909 align:start position:0%
 
poaching has been an illegal practice  for hundreds of years  but it was during the late middle ages  that poaching became a punishable  offence  during this time the right to hunt was  limited to landowners  and royalty in the 1900s  the new european colonial states enacted  preservation laws that would stop the  majority of africans from hunting  this meant that most forms including the  hunting of food were officially deemed  as poaching  however this was the time where  commercial poaching started to become  prevalent  and a threat to animal populations  after independence was gained in the  1950s and 60s  most african countries retained these  laws but poaching for food continued  as well as the poaching for commercial  gain those hunting for food present a  threat to animal populations  but not on the same level as those who  did so for international markets  in the early 2000s the asian demand for  ivory began to rise steeply  and poaching in africa rose to crisis  levels  the impact of poaching isn't limited to  the endangerment and extinction of  animals being hunted  earth's ecosystems are sensitive the  eradication of one species can lead to  the endangerment of another  smaller communities who rely on tourism  will quickly find themselves without  anything to show  but probably the least talked about  impact that poaching can have  is on the health of humanity across the  globe illegal poaching has been linked  to several recent diseases  that have heavily impacted the quality  of life across regions far beyond the  point of the initial hunt  for example the outbreak of sars in hong  kong has been traced to the human  contact  and consumption of poached meat  available on the black wildlife markets  ebola one of the world's most horrific  diseases has had outbreaks in africa  directly linked to the poaching and  consumption  of primates and bird flu monkey pox  and heartwater disease are examples of  diseases directly facilitated by the  illegal wildlife trade  according to the u.s center for disease  control 75 percent of diseases  are zoonotic and reach humans through  animals  and given that illegal smugglers won't  exactly be concerned about effectively  monitoring and quarantining their  international imports  this is an incredibly difficult problem  to control  government efforts to combat poaching is  vastly under-resourced  and ineffective but in recent times  there have been numerous local  community-based groups that have stood  up  and established themselves as national  caretakers of the animal reserves  these organizations have had to ensure  that their training and equipment  is up to the task of keeping up to the  speed with the forever evolving nature  of those involved with the illegal trade  after the reactivation of team rainbow  in 2015 one of these caretakers was  recruited to bring her skills and  expertise to assist in the fight against  terrorism on a global scale  this operator was named melusi  a true predator strikes out of necessity  not  greed and they protect what is theirs  [Music]  fandue and love codenamed melusi  is a defending operator in team rainbow  born in the small south african town  of loewsburg near the athala game  reserve malusi spent many years  exploring the rugged surrounding  landscape with a friend  elna gardner initially brought together  after finding out that both of their  names essentially meant the same thing  beloved they quickly discovered that  they also had a shared passion for the  animals of the game reserve  as they grew up their sense of  appreciation and responsibility for the  animals only increased  they saw the devastation to the park's  animal population as a result of the  greed and cold indifference of the  poachers  every year the threat to the wildlife  rose and they knew that they had to take  action  although they could only save so many  with their limited resources  and the poachers and their victims were  countless  the inhumane and senseless slaughter  drove their determination  their focus and their passion to  concentrate solely on the lives they  could save  eventually elner went on to become an  accomplished veterinarian whereas malusi  chose the path of prevention  and thus joined the south african  national defence force  the role of the south african national  defence force is border protection  and preventing illegal immigration and  drug smuggling they also focus on  serious crimes  such as a tax on farms stock theft  prevention of trafficking drugs  weapons and ammunition car hijackings  and taxi violence  her aim was to broaden her skill set but  also to generate the funds she required  to pay for field ranger training  this one year course involves a number  of different eco-training camps in south  africa and botswala  which teaches its participants expert  knowledge of the african flora and fauna  as well as professional safari guiding  half of the training is spent as an  intern in a lodge to gain profound  hands-on guide experience she also  learned about geology  astronomy weather and climate animal  identification and behaviour  and conservation management  eventually she was noticed by the one  parachute battalion  the only full-time paratrooper unit of  the south african army  to give would-be members the endurance  and fitness they would need for  operations  in the harsh african conditions soldiers  were put through a grueling regime of  psychological and physical tests  including physical training three times  a day no walking in camp under any  circumstances  20 kilometer runs carrying tarp holes  dragging car tyres  carrying a 25 kg concrete slab  everywhere the candidate goes  night runs with 25 kilos of equipment  20 kilometer 75 kilo stretcher runs  digging trenches and the carrying of  artillery canisters  over 10 kilometers all while being timed  after the six month ordeal the select  few obtain their wings  but it doesn't stop here individuals are  required to continue honing their skills  in advanced driving demolitions tactics  and patrolling  unarmed combat survival skills escape  invasion  trafficking counter-insurgency  operations and  ambush and anti-ambush techniques  but unfortunately once active her time  with this unit was short-lived  winner battalion participated in the  south african military assistance to the  central african republic operation  ambushed by the seleka rebels the unit  suffered greatly  with 13 killed 27 injured and one  missing in action  malusi was one of the soldiers left  badly wounded by the event  and lucky to have escaped with her life  although the one parachute battalion  would eventually receive battle honours  for this operation a few years later  it's clear that this event has had a  huge impact on her even today she still  refuses to speak on what happened  and remains forever changed and haunted  by the incident  still in close contact elner eventually  reached out to melusi  asking for her help in defending malue  infilozy park  the oldest and only state-run nature  reserve in africa  home to africa's big five game elephant  rhinoceros  cape buffalo lion and leopard it was  faced with a rapidly growing poacher  threat  melusi resigned from the military and  began training local women  in trafficking interception and  surveillance  together they became the driving force  behind the incubator task force  anti-poaching unit  even after everything she had seen in  the defence force melusi seemed to be  more affected by what she witnessed on  the reserve  and armed with her extensive training  she tackled this new enemy with a  military-like precision  setbacks altercations and tragedy only  pushed her further  and this showed in the way that she  trained the woman and the new drone  surveillance systems that she rolled out  to assist in the fight but the poachers  were also evolving with the times  becoming more militarized they were  forever changing their tactics  and using more specialized equipment  this meant that malusi had to be highly  adaptive  she would wait intercept and prevent  in turn she would become the poacher of  poachers  this method was met with a number of  different reactions even if she wasn't  able to stop them herself  she would often slow them down enough to  be intercepted by others or even  discourage the poachers from continuing  altogether  melusi is a warden she watches she  defends  and she gets the job done she is  efficient  precise and understands that greed  remains one of humanity's greatest  challenges  melusi strongly believes that fighting  the poachers isn't enough  but that in order to move forward in  this fight that there must be education  and a shared responsibility within the  community  if the future is to ever really change  she is proud of the accomplishments that  a team of women have managed to achieve  and she believes they've truly made a  difference in the lives of the animals  they protect  she understands that the local economy  needs help but that this form of  acquiring easy money  isn't the way and in the long term  cannot be sustained  melusi's drive and determination is of  huge benefit to rainbow  her methods of focusing on the action of  prevention through surveillance  and putting an end to the incident  before it can occur will always be the  preferred method to picking up the  pieces after the devastation  [Music]  this one was a little different to the  other operators i've covered up until  now  although melusi came from a military  background the majority of the story  focused on her love and the need to  protect animals in the country she calls  home  so instead i focused on this this is  actually a particular area of interest  for me  the environment and especially the  animals i mean  i'm no greta not by a long shot but this  is probably the reason my house looks  like a petting zoo half the time  anyway thank you very much for watching  i hope you've enjoyed it and i'll catch  in the next one  cheers  [Music]  you

